#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "d6c73b7b-4981-5908-b694-be7f0e57b612" :question/math-academy-id "e-5490" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below.\nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3$, then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{2x_{1} - 6x_{3} = 0; 2x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 4x_{3} = 0; x_{1} - 3x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3$. From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3x_{3} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "b087e601-c054-563c-b7ee-43e18c74ac51" :question/math-academy-id "e-5491" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{3x_{1} + 3x_{2} + 4x_{3} = 0; 0 = 0; x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}↔R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0$. Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128877 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "c22ab531-3377-5827-93d2-a13854b3308c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5492" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$, where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nThus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=4\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4$. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} - 4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-3x_{1} = 0; - 5x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of an Eigenvector Corresponding to a Given Eigenvalue"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b110fbcf-44bb-5420-853c-add32278cfdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127035} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3ed5f-4293-5366-9531-1441ef61d5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127036} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3df827-8bee-5714-9485-43a88b2653a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127037} {:answer/id #uuid "29b9fca0-e7b2-50b2-81bc-2bcc2c08ccda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127038} {:answer/id #uuid "3edcdcbe-c517-5580-90ee-fce905803719" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec3ed5f-4293-5366-9531-1441ef61d5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127036} :answer-field/id #uuid "250520b3-0599-5f64-9ac4-a3d2b14632a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127034}] :question/id #uuid "dccac4c6-9a86-5592-a55b-31cd9ffa2f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-36814" :question/problem "$v = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above.\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 7$, then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5e7b10a-976e-52af-a34a-0d35b8cf9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127071} {:answer/id #uuid "edfa906c-ba0b-5d08-844e-2757672a35b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127072} {:answer/id #uuid "0837b43c-9204-5777-a4cf-a4b99e79346b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127073} {:answer/id #uuid "62875dbd-ee03-5f0b-a16b-0b37340bcb63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127074} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2ec017-1921-5014-bdc7-0ffed41fb1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a2ec017-1921-5014-bdc7-0ffed41fb1c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127075} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d93e8d4-30e3-5693-af9a-5d8f209511da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127070}] :question/id #uuid "4fce4c9f-341f-5617-8150-093fe040d196" :question/math-academy-id "q-37385" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-3$ and $λ_{2} = 2$ of the matrix $A$, given above. Find the third eigenvector $v_{3} = [a, b, c]^{T}$ such that the set ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"}]}
